{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "opponent next-possession PPP after offensive play type",
    "defensive aftershock from play type",
    "play-type AVOID labeling based on downstream opponent efficiency",
    "own-sequence lag effects on PPP",
    "sequential dependence in basketball possessions"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure opponent next-possession PPP conditional on own play type without lookahead or end-of-quarter bias?",
    "How to separate play-type-specific defensive aftershock from generic dead-ball or score effects?",
    "What sample sizes are needed to detect play-type-specific differences in opponent next-PPP given small effect sizes?",
    "How to control for confounding by lineup strength, score margin, period, and opponent quality?",
    "How reliable are inferred play-type labels for measuring downstream effects given known labeling artifacts?",
    "How to handle multiple testing across play types and own-sequence lags?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-075",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "4/8 play types show real downstream opponent suppression (CUT +0.0220, TRANSITION +0.0145, SPOT_UP +0.0124, POST_UP +0.0194 PPP), but effects are small (~0.01-0.02 PPP). DRIVE downstream not real. Player-level downstream mostly non-significant after BH correction."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF07",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "All 8 play types exhibit a defensive aftershock after success: opponent next-possession PPP is 0.12-0.14 lower than baseline. The aftershock is uniformly positive and may be a generic dead-ball/score effect rather than play-type-specific disruption. Own-next effects left as exploratory."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Play-type classifier assisted-flag leak caused SPOT_UP to show 2.83 PPP (100% makes) because assisted flag exists only on makes; after rewrite SPOT_UP dropped to ~1.16."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "OTHER play type inflated by foul endings (36% foul rate); DRIVE foul-draw rate 12pp below benchmark; initiation_type maps only 0.7% of possessions. Systematic labeling issues bias PPP estimates."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-014",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Play-type label accuracy audit overall FAIL: OTHER 20.2% vs <10% target; 40.8% low confidence; DRIVE foul-draw 1.7% vs 14-22% benchmark; most PTs PPP above benchmark band; MIA identity checks fail."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "what_failed": "using inferred play-type labels with assisted-flag leakage to estimate opponent next-PPP by play type",
      "why": "assisted flag only on made shots, inflating SPOT_UP PPP to 2.83, creating a C_artifact"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "what_failed": "relying on uncalibrated play-type labels for PPP comparisons",
      "why": "OTHER absorbs foul-ending possessions inflating PPP; DRIVE leaks foul-draw possessions; initiation_type covers <1% of possessions"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-014",
      "what_failed": "overall play-type label audit",
      "why": "failure to meet volume, foul-rate, and PPP benchmarks; high OTHER rate and low confidence indicate systematic labeling problems"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "A small but detectable play-type-specific opponent downstream suppression exists for some play types (~0.01-0.02 PPP; SF-APPS-075).",
    "A larger defensive aftershock (~0.12-0.14 PPP) appears after successful offensive possessions, but it is roughly uniform across play types and may be a generic dead-ball/set-defense effect rather than play-type-specific (SF07).",
    "Play-type labels inferred from play-by-play have had serious artifacts (assisted-flag leak, foul leakage, high OTHER rates) that can produce spurious PPP differences (SF-PAP-096, SF-CHT-025, SF-APPM-014).",
    "Own-sequence lags have not been rigorously tested beyond exploratory analysis (SF07)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether the play-type-specific downstream effects are large enough to warrant AVOID labels (current estimates are 0.01-0.02 PPP, likely smaller than direct PPP differences).",
    "Whether own-sequence lag effects exist after conditioning on opponent aftershock and score/personnel confounders.",
    "Whether the downstream effects are causal or confounded by lineup, score, or defensive scheme changes.",
    "Whether labeling issues have been fully resolved in the current version of the graph database; the audits may refer to earlier inference versions."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The small magnitude of play-type-specific downstream suppression (0.01-0.02 PPP) makes it unlikely that opponent next-PPP alone can usefully drive AVOID labels, as the signal is easily lost in noise.",
    "The large generic aftershock after made shots could be mistaken for a play-type effect if not properly conditioned on possession outcome, which would lead to spurious AVOID recommendations.",
    "Given the historical labeling failures, any analysis of play-type-level opponent next-PPP must first demonstrate that the current labels are free of the known artifacts, which is not yet shown."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Whether the play-type inference pipeline used in the current data has been fixed to eliminate the ARTIFACTs documented in SF-PAP-096 and SF-CHT-025.",
    "Whether opponent next-PPP effects differ meaningfully across score margin, player matchups, or defensive schemes (potential interaction effects unexplored).",
    "Sample sizes for rare play types (e.g., PUTBACK) may be insufficient even with four seasons of data to detect the small PT-specific component after conditioning on confounders.",
    "Whether own-sequence lags have any measurable signal independent of the generic aftershock."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no",
    "explanation": "The historical evidence confirms that the effect size for opponent next-PPP by play type is small and that labeling artifacts have been pervasive, but these findings are from similar exploratory work and do not resolve the core question of whether the effect is large enough for AVOID labeling. The decision to proceed or not is driven by the methodological assessment, which the history strongly informs."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.35,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "Inferred play-type labels have a history of systematic errors (assisted-flag leak, foul leakage, high OTHER rate) that can bias PPP estimates and downstream analysis. It is unknown whether the current labels meet quality thresholds."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent next-PPP is heavily confounded by whether the preceding possession was a score, dead-ball, quarter, score margin, and lineup strength. A generic aftershock exists after made shots that must be separated from play-type-specific effects."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "To detect a 0.02 PPP difference with 80% power requires ~40k observations per play type per condition; rare play types (PUTBACK ~6k after success per SF07) may fall short once conditioning on confounders."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPS-075",
      "FAM:SF07",
      "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "FAM:SF-APPM-014"
    ],
    "summary": "SF-APPS-075 found small but real opponent downstream suppression for 4/8 play types (~0.02 PPP). SF07 found a larger but generic defensive aftershock (~0.13 PPP) after successful possessions. Multiple artifact records (SF-PAP-096, SF-CHT-025, SF-APPM-014) show that inferred play-type labels have suffered from severe biases that inflate PPP and misclassify possessions. Own-sequence lag effects remain largely unexplored."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Play-type labels are inferred and have a documented history of artifacts (assisted-flag leak, foul leakage, misclassification of DRIVE/OTHER). Without a fresh audit showing label quality is adequate, any downstream PPP analysis risks reproducing biased estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent next-PPP is strongly influenced by whether the preceding possession ended in a make, dead-ball, quarter ending, free throws, or was a live-ball turnover, as well as score margin, period, and lineup strength. Failure to fully condition on these will alias generic effects as play-type-specific."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The expected PT-specific effects are ~0.01-0.02 PPP, requiring very large samples. Rare play types (PUTBACK, CUT) and conditioning on matchups or game states will reduce effective sample below what is needed for reliable inference."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If AVOID labels are derived from the same data on which the test is conducted, the analysis risks circularity and overfitting, with no out-of-sample validation of the decision rule."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing multiple play types, own-sequence lags, and interactions inflates false discovery risk. Correction is needed but reduces power further."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Strategic and rule changes across seasons (2022-23 through 2025-26) may cause the relationship between play type and opponent next-PPP to drift, making a single pooled estimate misleading."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.1,
    "rejected": 0.1,
    "untestable": 0.3,
    "artifact": 0.25,
    "uncertain": 0.25
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "Average treatment effect of an offensive team running a specific play type X on the opponent's next-possession points per possession, estimated as E[OppNextPPP | PlayType=X, Success=1] - E[OppNextPPP | PlayType=X, Success=1, matched controls], controlling for score margin, period, seconds remaining, lineup strength, home/away, and possession-outcome type (make, dead-ball, live-ball turnover), with a separate estimation for own-next PPP lag.",
    "unit": "One offensive possession ending in a field-goal attempt, turnover, or shooting foul (excluding end-of-quarter possessions with no following defensive possession, technical free throws, and heaves).",
    "sample": "All possessions from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season and playoffs where the play-type label confidence exceeds a validated threshold and the label is not OTHER. Minimum 30 observations per cell after conditioning. Possessions with ambiguous or conflicting labels are excluded or used for placebo testing.",
    "power": "To detect a 0.02 PPP effect with 80% power at alpha=0.05/8 (Bonferroni for 8 play types) assuming opponent PPP SD ~1.0, requires ~60,000 observations per play type in the success condition. Available n after success for common play types (PNR, SPOT_UP, DRIVE) exceeds this (50k+ per SF07) but marginal for CUT (~21k) and insufficient for PUTBACK (~6k). Reduced power for interaction analyses.",
    "controls": "Exact score margin (bucketed), period, seconds remaining (bucketed), home indicator, a 10-man lineup strength term (sum of player plus-minus or RAPM estimates aggregated at time of possession), defensive team indicator, possession-outcome fixed effects (made shot, missed shot with offensive rebound, dead-ball turnover, live-ball turnover, shooting foul), and an after-timeout indicator.",
    "falsification": [
      "Placebo test: Repeat analysis using only possessions with low-confidence play-type labels or randomly shuffled labels; the observed PT-specific effects should vanish.",
      "Generic aftershock test: Show that the PT-specific component does not simply replicate the uniform aftershock after made shots; test whether the residual after controlling for possession outcome is zero for 'neutral' play types.",
      "Temporal stability: Split the sample into half-seasons and test whether PT-specific downstream effects are stable across time; instability suggests non-stationarity or overfitting.",
      "Out-of-sample validation: Train a labeling decision rule on 2022-23 through 2024-25 and test whether recommended AVOID play types predict worse opponent next-PPP in 2025-26 holdout."
    ],
    "multiplicity": "Primary test: 8 play-type-specific tests for opponent next-PPP after success. Secondary: 8 for own-next PPP lag. Use Benjamini-Hochberg FDR control at 0.1 across all 16 tests. Additional exploratory breakdowns by score margin or opponent strength are not used for labeling decisions.",
    "decision_rule": "A play type is labeled AVOID if (1) its causal effect on opponent next-PPP is positive (i.e., opponent scores more) and statistically significant at FDR<0.1, (2) the effect magnitude exceeds 0.03 PPP (practical significance threshold), (3) the effect passes the temporal stability falsification test, and (4) the recommendation is directionally consistent in the holdout season. If no play type meets all criteria, the analysis concludes insufficient evidence to support AVOID labeling from downstream opponent PPP.",
    "data_version": "Play-by-play data freeze: NBA stats API snapshot as of 2026-06-19. Play-type inference pipeline version must be audited and confirmed free of the assisted-flag leak (SF-PAP-096) and foul leakage (SF-CHT-025) artifacts, with OTHER rate below 10% and confidence distributions comparable across play types. The audit must be documented and referenced as a gate condition before estimating the primary effects."
  }
}}

